# RULE_PRIORITY_DAEM Call Relationships

Start with the constant block at the top of `mobile/src/main/rust/vpnhotspotd/src/routing.rs`. Line 40 is `RULE_PRIORITY_DAEMON_BASE`, followed by three consecutive upstream-related base values. Keep all four lines together when searching to avoid missing the fallback or disable-system branches by searching only for the daemon name.

```rust
const RULE_PRIORITY_DAEMON_BASE: u32 = 20600;
const RULE_PRIORITY_UPSTREAM_BASE: u32 = 20700;
const RULE_PRIORITY_UPSTREAM_FALLBACK_BASE: u32 = 20800;
const RULE_PRIORITY_UPSTREAM_DISABLE_SYSTEM_BASE: u32 = 20900;
```

The four declared values increase in file order, in steps of 100. When tracing references, note the full constant name and whether the referencing expression adds an offset to the base value.

Below the constant block are `IpOperation`, `IpFamily`, and `RouteType`, in that order, on lines 46, 52, and 58. Read these nearby types first, then return to the constant references to view the priority names and routing objects in the same context.

The file header also includes `HashSet`, `io`, `IpAddr`, `Ipv4Addr`, `Ipv6Addr`, and `cidr::Ipv4Inet`. When filling in references later, start by searching for the full symbol names.

# MAX_CONTROL_PACKET Call Flow Guide

Start by opening `mobile/src/main/rust/vpnhotspotd/src/control.rs`. MAX_CONTROL_PACKET is only a name used for lookup in this record; at the time, it was not assigned to a network entry point, and the caller was not recorded.

The file header shows the following dependencies:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::ffi::OsStr;
use std::io;
use std::os::unix::ffi::OsStrExt;
```

When reading around the constants, first check whether the limit value enters this file's control branch. `HashMap` alone is insufficient to show that the packet is cached, and `OsStrExt` cannot be directly linked to control-packet encoding either.

Open questions: Is the limit checked inside control.rs, or supplied by the caller first? It has also not been recorded whether, after the limit is triggered, the flow returns through `io`.

# MAX_ERROR_DETAILS Event Output Notes

Entry point: `mobile/src/main/rust/vpnhotspotd/src/shared/protocol.rs`.

The initial draft recorded `MAX_ERROR_DETAILS` as a name to search for near the event output point. The dependencies visible at the top of the file are:

```rust
use std::error::Error;
use std::fmt;
use std::io;
use std::net::{IpAddr, Ipv4Addr, Ipv6Addr};
```

Start with the declarations in this file, then look at how the error object reaches the output point through formatting and the I/O type. `IpAddr`, `Ipv4Addr`, and `Ipv6Addr` are another group of local objects in the same file; they cannot simply be lumped together with the detail limit.

The callers of `MAX_ERROR_DETAILS` have not yet been confirmed, nor has it been confirmed whether the limit is applied before detail assembly or before output. Whether the event output point accepts only the values defined here is also unverified.

